"""Improve an existing purchase decision; no new expansion URL."""
import expand_hold_period as renderer

renderer.SLUG='str-revenue-guarantee-offers'
renderer.TITLE='Guaranteed STR Rent: Test the Offer Before Buying'
renderer.H1='Should you accept a guaranteed rent offer for your short-term rental?'
renderer.DESC='Before buying an STR with promised rent, compare owner-paid costs, operator payment risk and transition cash using a like-for-like acquisition worksheet.'
renderer.FAQ=[
('Does guaranteed rent make an STR purchase risk-free?', 'No. The obligation depends on the agreement, the responsible entity, its ability to pay and applicable conditions. Verify the operating plan and fund a payment-interruption case before relying on the offer.'),
('Can I compare promised rent directly with forecast guest revenue?', 'No. Put both alternatives on the same owner-cash basis. Deduct the costs the owner retains, distinguish reserves from expenses and show debt service and taxes consistently.'),
('Does a seller’s operator agreement automatically continue for the buyer?', 'Do not assume so. Have counsel review the proposed transaction and obtain the operator’s accepted buyer terms, required consents and effective dates. Review permissions, insurance and lender requirements independently.'),
('Does a security deposit cover every missed payment?', 'No. Amount, custody, lawful use, competing obligations and timing must be established. Do not count an unverified deposit or possible legal recovery as accessible purchase cash.')]
renderer.BODY='''<p class="lead">If a property is being sold with an operator's promised monthly rent, underwrite the property and the payment obligation separately. A fixed figure on a sales sheet does not establish collectible income, permitted STR use or a funded fallback. Before offering, compare ordinary managed operation and the proposed operator arrangement on the same owner-cash basis. Then ask whether you could carry the purchase if payments stop. This guide supplies a responsibility register and labeled examples, not a recommendation to accept a particular lease or revenue guarantee.</p>
<p><a href="/apply/">Discuss the acquisition with both operating cases and a funded downside budget</a>. BNB Accelerator assists within its contracted acquisition scope; independent counsel, lenders, insurers and tax advisers establish their respective conclusions.</p>
<h2>Identify the obligation, not just the marketing label</h2>
<p>A proposal may involve a lease under which an operator owes stated rent, a management agreement with a conditional payout floor, or a hybrid. Have counsel identify the contracting parties, calculation, conditions, deductions, payment dates and enforcement provisions. Do not assume every offer called guaranteed rent gives the same rights or leaves the owner with no operating responsibilities.</p>
<p>Ask whether the seller's arrangement continues after your purchase, requires consent or must be replaced by a buyer agreement. Record when accepted buyer terms take effect, not simply when someone expects to introduce the manager. The <a href="/blog/assign-str-management-contract-sale/">manager-consent and handoff worksheet</a> covers continuity; this page addresses the offered payment and the acquisition's owner-cash comparison.</p>
<p>Request the complete agreement and amendments, a dated buyer proposal, authorized payment-history evidence and the identity of the entity responsible for paying. Distinguish verified earlier payments from future promises. A parent-company name, principal's reputation or marketing badge is not a reviewed enforceable guarantee. Ask counsel what an actual guarantee covers and financial reviewers what evidence supports the obligor's ability to pay. Keep sensitive contracts and financial records in agreed secure channels.</p>
<h2>Assign every cost before comparing income</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Item to reconcile</th><th>Managed-property case</th><th>Operator offer</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Revenue and payment base</td><td>Supported guest receipts on a defined period and basis</td><td>Contracted owner payment, conditions and permitted deductions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Channels, cleaning and utilities</td><td>Show charges and reimbursements without double counting</td><td>Identify the payer and exceptions in the actual agreement</td></tr>
<tr><td>Repairs, furnishings and capital work</td><td>Separate current bills from earmarked reserve money</td><td>Identify owner-retained work, approvals and return condition</td></tr>
<tr><td>Property taxes, insurance and debt</td><td>Show consistently in the final owner-cash bridge</td><td>Do not remove obligations merely because rent is fixed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deposit and other security</td><td>Do not treat restricted funds as income</td><td>Verify custody, lawful application and availability</td></tr>
<tr><td>Failure and transition</td><td>Model supported operating downside</td><td>Price payment interruption and a lawful replacement plan</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Add document location, responsible reviewer, due date and unresolved assumption to each row. Do not use one route's gross revenue against another route's payment after the operator absorbs several costs. A cash budget that includes a capital reserve allocation is not automatically net operating income: earmarking money does not incur an expense. Label the metric and show how it connects to accessible cash.</p>
<h2>Worked comparison on the same owner-cash basis</h2>
<p>Hypothetical planning amounts only, not client results, operator prices, market averages or lender qualifying income. Assume ordinary management produces $90,000 of annual guest receipts and $50,000 of operating cash outflows and planned allocations, including management, channel and cleaning costs, utilities, routine work and a capital reserve allocation. Assume those categories are distinct, no receipt or bill is counted twice, and property tax, insurance and debt service are excluded from that $50,000 subtotal.</p>
<p>For the alternative, assume an operator owes $3,000 monthly, or $36,000 annually, and pays the channel, cleaning, utility and routine operating categories. The owner still budgets $6,000 of distinct repair and capital cash needs plus the same $10,000 property-tax/insurance and $24,000 debt-service amounts. These responsibilities are invented for comparison; replace them with accepted terms and property-specific quotes.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Illustrative route</th><th>Consistent owner-cash budget</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Ordinary management, base case</td><td>$90,000 − $50,000 − $10,000 − $24,000 = $6,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Operator, all stated payments received</td><td>$36,000 − $6,000 − $10,000 − $24,000 = −$4,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Managed downside: $75,000 receipts, $43,000 operating cash budget</td><td>$75,000 − $43,000 − $10,000 − $24,000 = −$2,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Operator: three monthly payments not received in the modeled year</td><td>$27,000 − $6,000 − $10,000 − $24,000 = −$13,000</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The $10,000 difference between the two base cases is not a universal price of certainty. Changing documented responsibilities, the supported revenue case or financing changes the answer. Nor is a contractual payment the same as received cash. The interrupted-payment case replaces $36,000 with $27,000; it does not deduct the same missed payments again. It does not assume debt, insurance or tax bills stop because the operator fails to pay.</p>
<p>These are owner-cash planning balances after stated debt service and planned allocations, before income tax and omitted obligations—not NOI, taxable income or total investment profit. Reserve allocations are retained cash until spent. The managed downside's $43,000 subtotal is also invented, not a claim that costs automatically fall with bookings. Rebuild the downside with actual fixed and variable costs rather than applying an arbitrary expense percentage. Use the <a href="/underwriting/downside-scenario/">downside framework</a> for the broader purchase.</p>
<h2>Can the buyer fund interruption and transition?</h2>
<p>In a separate hypothetical liquidity check, suppose $30,000 accessible cash remains after closing and launch, with a $20,000 minimum retained buffer. Assume the modeled $13,000 annual interrupted-payment deficit consists of distinct actual outflows for this cash check, rather than unspent reserve allocations. Add $8,000 of separate transition bills not already included in that deficit. Accessible cash falls to $9,000, which is $11,000 below the chosen buffer. Sequence the actual payment dates as well: a year-end balance cannot fund an earlier bill.</p>
<p>Do not add an operator's security deposit, possible court recovery or an expected tax refund unless lawful access, amount and timing are established. The $8,000 transition amount is not automatically the seller's or operator's liability. Have counsel review notice, access, guest obligations, return condition, dispute procedures and what happens on sale or default. A payment gap does not itself give the buyer an immediate lawful right to take over listings or remove an operator.</p>
<h2>Confirm permission, financing and insurance independently</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.airbnb.com/help/article/1397">Airbnb's general hosting guidance</a> calls for checking relevant contracts and permissions and local requirements. It is a platform starting point, not parcel approval or a nationwide rule about operator leases. Resolve the proposed ownership, operator and guest use with the appropriate local authority and counsel. Do not infer transferability from the seller's existing listing or permit; use the <a href="/underwriting/permit-transfer/">permit-transfer guide</a> for that separate gate.</p>
<p><a href="https://content.naic.org/article/consumer-insight-renting-out-your-home-you-need-insurance-coverage-home-sharing-rentals">NAIC's home-sharing insurance guidance</a> warns that rental use can affect coverage and recommends discussing it with the insurer. Obtain policy-specific review of the actual operator arrangement, insured parties, exclusions and responsibilities. A certificate or platform protection is not a substitute for reviewing effective coverage. No particular endorsement or policy type is prescribed here.</p>
<p>Ask the lender how the proposed operator arrangement and owner payment affect eligibility, income recognition, documents and continuing covenants. Do not describe the invented $36,000 payment as lender-approved income. Ask the CPA to evaluate the actual activity and owner responsibilities separately; a fixed-rent label, high W-2 income or management agreement does not prove tax suitability or a deductible loss.</p>
<h2>Make a purchase decision, not a guarantee bet</h2>
<p>Proceed only when the permitted buyer arrangement, reviewed obligations, supported economics and funded failure case fit your limits. Reprice when verified retained costs or transition needs change a viable purchase. Seek a protected delay for decisive unanswered questions before the real contract deadline; do not assume extension or cancellation rights. Decline an acquisition that depends on unsupported collectibility or leaves the buyer unable to carry the asset.</p>
<p><a href="/apply/">Bring the agreement, responsibility register and both cash cases to an acquisition call</a>. The <a href="/blog/buy-str-high-income-large-tax-bill/">high-income buyer roadmap</a> connects operating choices to the rest of diligence. This comparison does not promise BNB Accelerator will arrange an operator, guarantee income or perform legal, lending or tax review.</p>
<p class="small">Primary sources reviewed October 7, 2026. NAIC's cited article is dated March 19, 2020; it is not newly issued 2026 guidance. Educational information only, not personalized investment, legal, tax, lending or insurance advice. All example amounts are hypothetical. No rent receipt, permission, service continuity, financing, tax benefit or investment result is guaranteed.</p>'''

if __name__=='__main__':
 renderer.main('2026-09-24','data-guaranteed-rent-review-link','Buying with an operator payment promise? Use the <a href="/blog/str-revenue-guarantee-offers/">guaranteed-rent owner-cost and interruption worksheet</a> before treating contracted rent as collectible income or available purchase cash.')
